fix(hook): refuse a capture the stopping run cannot account for (#261)

The stop-time capture signed everything uncommitted in the tree under the
stopping run. Measured three times on 2026-09-18: a read-only review subagent
signed 12 files and ~890 lines it had only read (457ca48); a second capture took
another agent's in-flight work; a third took a one-line edit the session made.
Each verifies against Fulcio and Rekor under the wrong identity, which is worse
than unsigned.

The first attempt bounded what the stop ADDS and left the commit unbounded. It
passed 27 assertions and let a nine-file capture through days later, because
`git add` removes nothing and the capture commits the INDEX: anything another
party had already staged went in regardless of what the bound picked.

So the bound is now on the commit. capture_account asks one question of the
index as it actually stands — is every path in it one this run's own tool-call
bodies record it writing — and if the answer is no the WHOLE capture is refused
(issue #261, option 3). It is asked twice: before anything is staged, so a
refusal leaves the tree untouched, and again on the index about to be committed.

Nothing is guessed out of a `Bash` command. The measured run made 62 Bash calls,
27 of them writing to a file, so the record is structurally incomplete — but
parsing redirections to recover the filenames takes a shell grammar this file
cannot evaluate and turns a guess into a signature that verifies forever under a
named identity. A run whose writes went through Bash accounts for nothing and
its capture is refused instead.

A refusal changes nothing on disk and names the run, the tree and the command
that signs the work under it later. Stranding the work silently would be worse
than the misattribution this removes.

Driven by OPS-074 (an index holding work the run cannot account for produces no
commit), OPS-075 (a run that recorded no write captures nothing in a dirty
tree), OPS-076 (a refusal is nameable) and OPS-077 (the capture that is allowed
is still bounded to the run's own writes). All four drive the shim against a
real repository and assert what reached a commit, which is the half the previous
tests never asked: they observed 57 passed / 7 failed against the old code, with
OPS-075 reproducing the read-only incident exactly. 56 cases before, 64 after.

shim-selftest.sh also pins INNSEGL_API_URL at a dead port, so the capture path
no longer branches on whether this machine's deployment happens to be up.

